const COURSE = [
{title:'First words',jp:'はじめの一歩',icon:'sun',color:'#E9B96B',goal:'Greet someone and keep a conversation going.',lessons:[
['A little hello','Greet people at different times of day.','Japanese vowels stay consistent: a (ah), i (ee), u (oo), e (eh), o (oh). A long vowel lasts an extra beat. The は in こんにちは is pronounced wa.',[
['こんにちは','Konnichiwa','Hello','Use during the daytime.'],['おはようございます','Ohayō gozaimasu','Good morning','A polite morning greeting.'],['こんばんは','Konbanwa','Good evening','Use when greeting someone in the evening.'],['ありがとうございます','Arigatō gozaimasu','Thank you','A polite everyday thank-you.']]],
['Your most useful word','Get attention and respond politely.','すみません can mean “excuse me” or a light apology. Use it to get a staff member’s attention. A small bow often accompanies greetings.',[
['すみません','Sumimasen','Excuse me','Also useful for a small apology.'],['はい','Hai','Yes','Also signals that you are listening.'],['いいえ','Iie','No','For a gentle refusal, add a thank-you.'],['大丈夫です','Daijōbu desu','It is okay','Context matters: this may also mean “no, thank you.”']]],
['Keep it going','Ask for slower speech or repetition.','もう一度 means “one more time.” ゆっくり means “slowly.” These repair phrases help you keep using Japanese even when you miss something.',[
['もう一度お願いします','Mō ichido onegai shimasu','One more time, please','Ask someone to repeat.'],['ゆっくりお願いします','Yukkuri onegai shimasu','Slowly, please','Ask someone to slow down.'],['日本語が少しわかります','Nihongo ga sukoshi wakarimasu','I understand a little Japanese','少し means “a little.”'],['わかりません','Wakarimasen','I do not understand','A useful honest response.']]]]},
{title:'Nice to meet you',jp:'はじめまして',icon:'smile',color:'#9DB29F',goal:'Introduce yourself and make a simple connection.',lessons:[
['Meet someone','Introduce yourself politely.','[Name] です means “I am [name].” You can omit 私は (watashi wa, “as for me”) when it is clear who you mean. Replace ジョセフ with your own name.',[
['はじめまして','Hajimemashite','Nice to meet you','Use at a first meeting.'],['ジョセフです','Josefu desu','I am Joseph','Swap in your own name.'],['お名前は何ですか','Onamae wa nan desu ka','What is your name?','か makes a polite question.'],['よろしくお願いします','Yoroshiku onegai shimasu','Pleased to meet you','A conventional phrase after an introduction.']]],
['Where are you from?','Talk about your home and trip.','[Place] から来ました means “I came from [place].” から marks the starting point. です is a polite ending; か turns the sentence into a question.',[
['マレーシアから来ました','Marēshia kara kimashita','I am from Malaysia','Replace マレーシア with a country.'],['クアラルンプールに住んでいます','Kuararunpūru ni sunde imasu','I live in Kuala Lumpur','に marks where you live.'],['旅行で来ました','Ryokō de kimashita','I came for a holiday','で gives the purpose or circumstance here.'],['どこから来ましたか','Doko kara kimashita ka','Where are you from?','どこ asks “where.”']]],
['A bit about you','Share a simple interest.','[Thing] が好きです means “I like [thing].” Replace 日本料理 with ラーメン (ramen) or 音楽 (ongaku, music). To ask, add か.',[
['日本料理が好きです','Nihon ryōri ga suki desu','I like Japanese food','が marks the thing you like.'],['仕事は何ですか','Shigoto wa nan desu ka','What do you do for work?','仕事 means work.'],['エンジニアです','Enjinia desu','I am an engineer','A job noun plus です.'],['日本は初めてです','Nihon wa hajimete desu','It is my first time in Japan','初めて means the first time.']]]]},
{title:'Your first order',jp:'いただきます',icon:'bowl',color:'#D6A099',goal:'Order a meal and make a polite request.',lessons:[
['A table, please','Enter a restaurant and ask for a menu.','Japanese uses different counters for people. 一人 (hitori) is one person; 二人 (futari) is two people. お願いします makes a request polite.',[
['一人です','Hitori desu','Just one person','Tell staff your party size.'],['二人です','Futari desu','Two people','For a table for two.'],['メニューをお願いします','Menyū o onegai shimasu','A menu, please','を marks the thing requested.'],['英語のメニューはありますか','Eigo no menyū wa arimasu ka','Do you have an English menu?','ありますか asks if something exists.']]],
['This one, please','Order food and drinks.','[Item] をください means “please give me [item].” これ means “this” near you. を is pronounced o. Try replacing 水 with コーヒー (kōhī, coffee).',[
['これをください','Kore o kudasai','This one, please','Point to a menu item.'],['水をください','Mizu o kudasai','Water, please','A direct, polite request.'],['ラーメンを一つお願いします','Rāmen o hitotsu onegai shimasu','One ramen, please','一つ is a general counter for one item.'],['おすすめは何ですか','Osusume wa nan desu ka','What do you recommend?','おすすめ means recommendation.']]],
['Make it your own','Ask about ingredients and settle the bill.','[Ingredient] は入っていますか asks if an ingredient is included. [Ingredient] なしでお願いします requests it without that ingredient. Confirm allergies with staff; an omission request alone is not an allergy check.',[
['肉は入っていますか','Niku wa haitte imasu ka','Does it contain meat?','肉 means meat.'],['ねぎなしでお願いします','Negi nashi de onegai shimasu','Without spring onion, please','なし means without.'],['お会計をお願いします','Okaikei o onegai shimasu','The bill, please','A common request at the end of a meal.'],['ごちそうさまでした','Gochisōsama deshita','Thank you for the meal','Say after eating or as you leave.']]]]},
{title:'Getting around',jp:'次はどこ？',icon:'train',color:'#93AFBD',goal:'Find your train and ask for directions.',lessons:[
['Where is the station?','Ask where a place is.','[Place] はどこですか means “where is [place]?” は is pronounced wa as the topic particle. Swap in コンビニ (konbini, convenience store).',[
['駅はどこですか','Eki wa doko desu ka','Where is the station?','駅 means train station.'],['トイレはどこですか','Toire wa doko desu ka','Where is the toilet?','Useful in any building.'],['ここですか','Koko desu ka','Is it here?','ここ means here.'],['地図を見せてください','Chizu o misete kudasai','Please show me a map','見せてください means please show me.']]],
['On the right track','Check your train and ticket.','[Destination] に行きますか asks if a vehicle goes to a place. に marks the destination. 何番線 (nanbansen) asks for a train platform number.',[
['この電車は東京駅に行きますか','Kono densha wa Tōkyō eki ni ikimasu ka','Does this train go to Tokyo Station?','この means “this” before a noun.'],['何番線ですか','Nanbansen desu ka','Which platform is it?','Listen for a number plus 番線.'],['切符はどこで買えますか','Kippu wa doko de kaemasu ka','Where can I buy a ticket?','どこで asks where an action happens.'],['片道でお願いします','Katamichi de onegai shimasu','One way, please','片道 means one-way.']]],
['Follow the directions','Understand simple directions.','まっすぐ means straight ahead; 右 is right; 左 is left. The て-form plus ください makes a polite instruction. Listen for landmarks too.',[
['まっすぐ行ってください','Massugu itte kudasai','Please go straight','行って is the linking form of go.'],['右に曲がってください','Migi ni magatte kudasai','Please turn right','右 means right.'],['左に曲がってください','Hidari ni magatte kudasai','Please turn left','左 means left.'],['歩いて何分ですか','Aruite nanpun desu ka','How many minutes on foot?','歩いて means by walking.']]]]},
{title:'Numbers & time',jp:'いくつですか',icon:'clock',color:'#C1AED0',goal:'Understand prices, quantities, and meeting times.',lessons:[
['Count on it','Recognise numbers used in daily life.','Say numbers as units: 十 (jū, ten), 百 (hyaku, hundred), 千 (sen, thousand). 300 is sanbyaku; 600 is roppyaku; 800 is happyaku. Counters can change pronunciation.',[
['一、二、三','Ichi ni san','One, two, three','Count the three beats separately.'],['四、五、六','Yon go roku','Four, five, six','よん is a common reading of four.'],['七、八、九、十','Nana hachi kyū jū','Seven, eight, nine, ten','なな is a common reading of seven.'],['千円です','Sen en desu','It is one thousand yen','円 is pronounced en.']]],
['What time?','Ask for the time and opening hours.','[Number] 時 means [number] o’clock. Four o’clock is yoji; seven is shichiji; nine is kuji. から and まで mean from and until.',[
['今何時ですか','Ima nanji desu ka','What time is it now?','今 means now.'],['三時です','Sanji desu','It is three o’clock','三時 is three o’clock.'],['何時からですか','Nanji kara desu ka','What time does it start?','から marks the start.'],['何時までですか','Nanji made desu ka','Until what time?','まで marks the endpoint.']]],
['Today or tomorrow?','Make a simple plan with a time.','Time words such as 今日 (today) and 明日 (tomorrow) often appear at the start. 行きます is “go”; 行きましょう is “let’s go.”',[
['今日行きます','Kyō ikimasu','I will go today','今日 can also refer to an action today.'],['明日行きます','Ashita ikimasu','I will go tomorrow','Japanese non-past forms also express future actions.'],['十時に会いましょう','Jūji ni aimashō','Let us meet at ten','に marks a specific time.'],['少し待ってください','Sukoshi matte kudasai','Please wait a moment','待って means wait.']]]]},
{title:'Stay a little',jp:'チェックイン',icon:'bed',color:'#B8BD91',goal:'Check in and ask for help at your hotel.',lessons:[
['Welcome to your stay','Check into your hotel.','予約 means reservation. [Name] で予約しています means you have a reservation under that name. At reception you may hear パスポート (passport).',[
['チェックインをお願いします','Chekkuin o onegai shimasu','I would like to check in','チェックイン is a loanword.'],['予約しています','Yoyaku shite imasu','I have a reservation','Use with your name.'],['ジョセフで予約しています','Josefu de yoyaku shite imasu','The reservation is under Joseph','で marks the name used for the booking.'],['パスポートです','Pasupōto desu','Here is my passport','Say while presenting it.']]],
['Make yourself at home','Ask about hotel facilities.','[Thing] はありますか asks “is there [thing]?” 何時ですか asks “what time?” Combine familiar patterns with new nouns.',[
['ワイファイはありますか','Waifai wa arimasu ka','Is there Wi-Fi?','Also often written Wi-Fi.'],['朝食は何時ですか','Chōshoku wa nanji desu ka','What time is breakfast?','朝食 means breakfast.'],['チェックアウトは何時ですか','Chekkuauto wa nanji desu ka','What time is checkout?','Use the same time pattern.'],['荷物を預けてもいいですか','Nimotsu o azukete mo ii desu ka','May I leave my luggage?','てもいいですか asks permission.']]],
['Something is not working','Explain a simple room problem.','[Appliance] が動きません means it does not work. [Thing] をください asks for something. Pointing and showing staff can support a simple explanation.',[
['エアコンが動きません','Eakon ga ugokimasen','The air conditioner is not working','動きません is a negative polite form.'],['お湯が出ません','Oyu ga demasen','There is no hot water','Literally: hot water does not come out.'],['タオルをください','Taoru o kudasai','A towel, please','Use a familiar request pattern.'],['部屋を変えられますか','Heya o kaeraremasu ka','Can I change rooms?','変えられますか asks whether a change is possible.']]]]},
{title:'Find a favourite',jp:'これ、いいですね',icon:'bag',color:'#D5AF80',goal:'Shop, ask about sizes, and pay.',lessons:[
['A little browsing','Ask about a price or an item.','これ is this near you; それ is that near the listener; あれ is that over there. この + noun means “this [noun].”',[
['これはいくらですか','Kore wa ikura desu ka','How much is this?','いくら asks the price.'],['これを見てもいいですか','Kore o mite mo ii desu ka','May I look at this?','てもいいですか asks permission.'],['見ているだけです','Mite iru dake desu','I am just looking','だけ means only.'],['ほかの色はありますか','Hoka no iro wa arimasu ka','Do you have another colour?','色 means colour.']]],
['The right fit','Ask to try something on.','もっと means more. 大きい is big and 小さい is small. In [adjective] の, の stands in for the item: “a bigger one.”',[
['試着してもいいですか','Shichaku shite mo ii desu ka','May I try it on?','試着 means trying on clothes.'],['もっと大きいのはありますか','Motto ōkii no wa arimasu ka','Do you have a bigger one?','大きい means big.'],['もっと小さいのはありますか','Motto chiisai no wa arimasu ka','Do you have a smaller one?','小さい means small.'],['これにします','Kore ni shimasu','I will take this one','A natural way to state your choice.']]],
['Ready to pay','Complete a purchase.','使えますか means “can I use [it]?” いりません means “I do not need [it].” Japanese often leaves out things already clear from context.',[
['カードは使えますか','Kādo wa tsukaemasu ka','Can I pay by card?','Literally: can a card be used?'],['現金で払います','Genkin de haraimasu','I will pay in cash','で marks the payment method.'],['袋はいりません','Fukuro wa irimasen','I do not need a bag','袋 means bag.'],['レシートをください','Reshīto o kudasai','A receipt, please','A familiar request with a new noun.']]]]},
{title:'Make a connection',jp:'お話ししましょう',icon:'chat',color:'#9FBFB3',goal:'Have a short exchange about your day and interests.',lessons:[
['How was it?','Describe a simple experience.','い-adjectives change for the past: おいしい → おいしかった. 楽しい → 楽しかった. Add です to keep the sentence polite.',[
['おいしいです','Oishii desu','It is delicious','Use about food or drink.'],['おいしかったです','Oishikatta desu','It was delicious','The past form.'],['楽しかったです','Tanoshikatta desu','It was fun','Talk about something you enjoyed.'],['とてもきれいですね','Totemo kirei desu ne','It is very beautiful, isn’t it?','ね invites agreement.']]],
['What do you like?','Ask and answer about interests.','何が好きですか asks what someone likes. も means “also.” 私もです is a short response when the shared meaning is clear.',[
['何が好きですか','Nani ga suki desu ka','What do you like?','何 is what.'],['音楽が好きです','Ongaku ga suki desu','I like music','Replace 音楽 with another interest.'],['私もです','Watashi mo desu','Me too','も means also.'],['いいですね','Ii desu ne','That sounds good','A friendly response to a suggestion.']]],
['Make a plan together','Suggest an activity and say goodbye.','[Verb stem] + ませんか makes a polite invitation: 行きませんか = “would you like to go?” ましょう means “let’s.”',[
['一緒に行きませんか','Issho ni ikimasen ka','Would you like to go together?','一緒に means together.'],['コーヒーを飲みましょう','Kōhī o nomimashō','Let us have coffee','飲みましょう is “let’s drink.”'],['また会いましょう','Mata aimashō','Let us meet again','A friendly farewell.'],['今日はありがとうございました','Kyō wa arigatō gozaimashita','Thank you for today','A polite way to close your time together.']]]]},
{title:'When plans change',jp:'困ったとき',icon:'help',color:'#C8A4A1',goal:'Explain a problem and ask for assistance.',lessons:[
['Find some help','Get help when you are lost.','[Thing] をなくしました means “I lost [thing].” 道に迷いました means you have lost your way. Show an address or map when possible.',[
['道に迷いました','Michi ni mayoimashita','I am lost','Use when you cannot find your way.'],['助けてください','Tasukete kudasai','Please help me','A direct call for help.'],['財布をなくしました','Saifu o nakushimashita','I lost my wallet','財布 means wallet.'],['交番はどこですか','Kōban wa doko desu ka','Where is the police box?','交番 is a local police box.']]],
['Health & food needs','Explain a health need simply.','[Body part] が痛いです means “[body part] hurts.” アレルギーがあります means “I have an allergy.” These phrases start a conversation; always confirm ingredients and staff understanding.',[
['気分が悪いです','Kibun ga warui desu','I feel unwell','A general statement of illness.'],['お腹が痛いです','Onaka ga itai desu','My stomach hurts','お腹 is stomach or abdomen.'],['病院に行きたいです','Byōin ni ikitai desu','I want to go to a hospital','たい expresses wanting to do something.'],['卵のアレルギーがあります','Tamago no arerugī ga arimasu','I have an egg allergy','Replace 卵 only with the correct allergen.']]],
['Change the plan','Ask to cancel or change a booking.','[Noun] を変更したいです means “I want to change [noun].” できますか asks whether an action is possible. Keep the booking details available.',[
['予約を変更したいです','Yoyaku o henkō shitai desu','I want to change my reservation','変更 means change.'],['キャンセルできますか','Kyanseru dekimasu ka','Can I cancel?','できますか means “is it possible?”'],['遅れます','Okuremasu','I will be late','Use with a time if you know it.'],['英語を話せる人はいますか','Eigo o hanaseru hito wa imasu ka','Is there someone who speaks English?','います is used for people and animals.']]]]},
{title:'Your own adventure',jp:'さあ、出かけよう',icon:'mountain',color:'#92AA92',goal:'Combine what you know into your own travel plans.',lessons:[
['What would you like to do?','Talk about things you want to do.','Verb stem + たいです means “I want to…”: 行きます → 行きたいです; 食べます → 食べたいです. どこに asks about a destination.',[
['京都に行きたいです','Kyōto ni ikitai desu','I want to go to Kyoto','Swap 京都 for another destination.'],['寿司を食べたいです','Sushi o tabetai desu','I want to eat sushi','食べたい means want to eat.'],['写真を撮ってもいいですか','Shashin o totte mo ii desu ka','May I take a photo?','Ask before photographing someone or a restricted area.'],['どこに行きたいですか','Doko ni ikitai desu ka','Where do you want to go?','Combine where with want to go.']]],
['Tell your story','Describe yesterday and tomorrow.','Polite verbs use ました for past and ません for negative. 行きます → 行きました. Natural Japanese often omits “I” when the speaker is clear.',[
['昨日京都に行きました','Kinō Kyōto ni ikimashita','I went to Kyoto yesterday','昨日 means yesterday.'],['明日は大阪に行きます','Ashita wa Ōsaka ni ikimasu','I will go to Osaka tomorrow','The non-past form expresses a plan.'],['お土産を買いました','Omiyage o kaimashita','I bought souvenirs','買いました is bought.'],['また日本に来たいです','Mata Nihon ni kitai desu','I want to come to Japan again','来たい is want to come.']]],
['Travel with confidence','Connect requests and close a conversation.','You now have reusable patterns: X はどこですか, X をください, X はありますか, X に行きたいです. Practise substituting nouns and responding aloud without looking.',[
['近くにコンビニはありますか','Chikaku ni konbini wa arimasu ka','Is there a convenience store nearby?','近くに means nearby.'],['ここまでお願いします','Koko made onegai shimasu','To here, please','Show a destination to a taxi driver.'],['とても助かりました','Totemo tasukarimashita','That was very helpful','Thank someone for practical help.'],['良い一日を','Yoi ichinichi o','Have a nice day','A friendly closing phrase.']]]]}
];
const KANA_ROWS=[['a','あいうえお','アイウエオ',['a','i','u','e','o']],['k','かきくけこ','カキクケコ',['ka','ki','ku','ke','ko']],['s','さしすせそ','サシスセソ',['sa','shi','su','se','so']],['t','たちつてと','タチツテト',['ta','chi','tsu','te','to']],['n','なにぬねの','ナニヌネノ',['na','ni','nu','ne','no']],['h','はひふへほ','ハヒフヘホ',['ha','hi','fu','he','ho']],['m','まみむめも','マミムメモ',['ma','mi','mu','me','mo']],['y','や ゆ よ','ヤ ユ ヨ',['ya','','yu','','yo']],['r','らりるれろ','ラリルレロ',['ra','ri','ru','re','ro']],['w','わ   を','ワ   ヲ',['wa','','','','wo']],['n','ん','ン',['n']],['g','がぎぐげご','ガギグゲゴ',['ga','gi','gu','ge','go']],['z','ざじずぜぞ','ザジズゼゾ',['za','ji','zu','ze','zo']],['d','だぢづでど','ダヂヅデド',['da','ji','zu','de','do']],['b','ばびぶべぼ','バビブベボ',['ba','bi','bu','be','bo']],['p','ぱぴぷぺぽ','パピプペポ',['pa','pi','pu','pe','po']]];
